                <figure class="post__image post__image--full"><img loading="lazy"  src="https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/1-Heads-back-2.jpg" alt="" width="2112" height="2334" sizes="(max-width: 48em) 100vw, 768px" srcset="https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-xs.jpg 300w ,https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-sm.jpg 480w ,https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-md.jpg 768w ,https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-lg.jpg 1024w ,https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-xl.jpg 1360w ,https://rebeen-art.github.io/rebeen/media/posts/13/responsive/1-Heads-back-2-2xl.jpg 1600w"></figure>
<p>Photos of the head: Soran Salam</p>
<p>2005 Head’s back</p>
<p>The work combines fifteen prints in various tones but the same original photographs of my head; my effort was to rebuild the part we missed because of culture. At the time, there was a big positivity toward anything related to Kurdish culture, but in the work I was trying to look at culture critically.</p>
<p><br>Children in Kurdistan were tied up inside a cradle when they were born until they became older and could no longer stay in the cradle; in this process, the soft part at the child's head's back becomes pressed and flattened. This depressed part of the head metaphorically leaves a bigger depression in humans when they grow, as they are effortfully trying to rebuild their lost part.</p>
<p>The work was part of the 2005 Project exhibition; at the time, it was a new effort from our younger generation to discover working with other media and concepts outside painting. </p>
<p> </p>
